= Navigation =

BITwiki navigation should support deliberate study, exploratory traversal, contribution, and maintenance across a semantic knowledge graph.

== Navigation is plural ==

No single tree should carry every job.

<pre>
Domain ≠ Portal ≠ Category ≠ Outline ≠ List ≠ Index ≠ Core/Vital set ≠ ontology
</pre>

The same subject can appear in several views because each answers a different question.

== Navigation surfaces ==

=== Main Page ===
The public entry point and concise orientation surface.

=== Portals ===
Reader-facing entry points into major fields or coherent knowledge surfaces.

Controlled Domain portals currently include:

* [[Portal:Systems science]] — foundational/integrative orientation
* [[Portal:Science]]
* [[Portal:Biology]]
* [[Portal:Computer science]]
* [[Portal:Mathematics]]
* [[Portal:Philosophy]]
* [[Portal:Technology]]
* [[Portal:Electronics]]
* [[Portal:Energy]]
* [[Portal:Engineering]]
* [[Portal:Chemistry]]
* [[Portal:Physics]]
* [[Portal:Medicine]]

=== Focused / recursive portals ===
A focused portal can exist below or across broad fields when it adds meaningful orientation.

Examples:

* [[Portal:Bioinformatics]] — parent contexts: Biology + Computer science
* [[Portal:Python]] — parent context: Computer science

Canonical portal identity should not depend on a filesystem-like path. Historical/convenience paths such as <code>Portal:Biology/Bioinformatics</code> may redirect to the canonical focused portal.

A portal relationship is navigation, not ontology inheritance. A child may represent a subfield, method, language, technology, interdisciplinary surface, or another coherent route into the corpus.

=== Reader depth ===
Portals and pages should support progressive entry:

'''Simple orientation → Core understanding → Technical depth → Frontier / research'''

See [[BITwiki:Readable depth and localization]]. This is primarily depth/readability localization; language translation is a separate dimension.

=== Categories ===
Human-readable browse/index groups. Subcategories may have multiple parents. A substantive category should explain Scope, Includes, Excludes/boundary, Category role, Parent/subcategories, important pages, and related navigation.

Category membership does not silently assign Entity type or Domain.

=== Outlines ===
Hierarchical study maps that expose the internal shape of a field without declaring that shape to be ontology.

=== Lists and registries ===
Use when members need annotation, comparison fields, dates, references, ordering, or context that a bare category cannot carry.

=== Glossaries and indices ===
Glossaries support vocabulary learning; indices optimize lookup.

=== Contents / broad directories ===
Human-readable maps across major knowledge and project surfaces.

=== Core / Vital coverage ===
A future curated coverage-priority layer can identify important missing or weak knowledge without turning importance into a Domain or Entity type. See [[BITwiki:Navigation and coverage research]].

=== Semantic queries and knowledge graphs ===
Computed/visual views over structured properties and relationships. Graphs are views over knowledge, not independent truth.

== Contribution and administration ==

Reader navigation and project-operation navigation should both be obvious without being conflated.

* [[BITwiki:Contributing]] — contribution pathway
* [[BITwiki:Requested knowledge]] — explicit missing/weak coverage queue
* [[BITwiki:Research references]] — external research source index
* [[BITwiki:Comparative wiki research register]] — knowledge-system research

Administration, maintenance, validation, governance, requests, drafts, portal/category administration, templates/properties, automation, and security belong in project-operational surfaces rather than subject Domains.

== Systems science orientation ==
Historical design placed Systems Science / Systems Theory as the top intellectual priority: individual knowledge domains are interpreted as interacting systems rather than isolated silos. This is an integrative worldview, not a demand that every page literally nest beneath one category.

== Design research ==
See [[BITwiki:Navigation and coverage research]] for comparative evidence, domain gaps, portal failure modes, category contracts, and future Core/Vital coverage research.

